AB  - Clinically, early dementia detection is crucial since mild cognitive and neurological impairments may arise years before diagnosis. AI-based systems ignore multimodal signal evolution across temporal instance sets and use singlemodality features or static categorization, notwithstanding their promise. Fragmented predictions rarely endure real-world drift. To fill this gap, our multi-stage analytical framework measures early dementia risk with temporal, structural, and interpretive coherence sets. Adaptive Multi-Modal Feature Distillation (AMFD) dynamically weights modalities by signal quality to build a compact 'dementia-risk vector' using MRI, EEG, cognitive-test, and behavioral data. TRTS uses these latent representations to find early risk pattern inflection points and temporal fingerprints before conversion. To boost training diversity without conversion samples, Counterfactual Synthetic Oversampled Cohort (CSOC) employs a generative model to create realistic trajectories. A graph-neural system called Hierarchical Explainable Risk-Graph Engine (HERGE) combines temporal and multimodal nodes to create therapeutically relevant sub-graphs from enriched data. The final component, Real-World Drift-Adaptive Revalidation (RFD-AR), checks model validity against simulated drift events during deployment. According to preliminary data, this chained framework increases prediction AUC from 0.80 to 0.92, diagnoses cognitive impairment nine months earlier than traditional tests, and maintains stability during sensor Variability in the Process. Laboratory precision and clinical practicality are combined in the study, suggesting adaptive, temporally aware validation may be the final step toward reliable AI-based dementia screening. © 2026 IEEE.
