AD  - Southern Marine Science and Engineering Guangdong Laboratory, Guangdong, Zhuhai, 519000, China
AB  - Modern recommender systems have achieved remarkable success in capturing user interests. However, their heavy reliance on correlations within observational data renders them susceptible to confounding biases, such as item popularity and exposure effects. These biases cause the model to learn spurious correlations between user behaviors and item attributes, thereby compromising fairness, novelty, and the ability to generalize to users’ true preferences. To address this fundamental challenge, we propose a novel debiasing framework, Causal-Guided Disentanglement for debiasing Recommendation (CGDR), which causally separates latent preference factors from confounding effects at the representation level. We first construct a Structural Causal Model (SCM) to explicitly delineate the generative process of user–item interactions, postulating that a user's action is determined by both their intrinsic preference and a set of confounding factors. Guided by this causal graph, our framework employs a novel Causal-Guided mechanism that leverages auxiliary supervisory tasks to provide the disentanglement process with a clear causal direction. This decomposes the latent representation into two independent components: one capturing robust, transferable true preferences, and the other explicitly modeling dynamic confounding effects. Upon achieving this disentanglement, we generate debiased recommendations via Counterfactual Reasoning, performing interventions on the confounder representation to simulate a bias-free environment while leveraging the preference representation for prediction. We demonstrate our framework using item popularity bias as a canonical and challenging example, and this framework is generalizable to other quantifiable confounders. Extensive experiments on multiple real-world datasets demonstrate that our proposed model not only significantly outperforms state-of-the-art baselines in recommendation accuracy, particularly in enhancing long-tail item performance, but also exhibits superior robustness and interpretability. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
