AD  - Samarkand State Medical University, Department of Internal Medicine No. 4, Samarkand, Uzbekistan
AB  - Over the last few decades, medical imaging has developed to play a key role in diagnostics, presenting objective, non-invasive, real-time, finely resolved information about organismal physiology. Although, in the last few years, deep learning techniques have parallelly developed for medical image recognition, and the introduction of such techniques also enhanced the field as well such that they improve the detection, classification, segmentation, and prognostication of several diverse diseases. The encompassment of deep leanings gives a ground-breaking analysis of this abstraction of images as they concern the transitioning of deep visions to diverse types of images like MRI, CT, US, X-ray or PET. By leveraging techniques such as convolutional neural networks, recurrent neural networks, generative adversarial networks and transformers, deep learning models have achieved phenomenal accuracy on tasks that were once highly reliant on radiologists' human interpretation. Example applications included its application for tumor detection and classification in brain magnetic resonance imaging, automated pneumonia detection in chest X-rays, liver segmentation from CT scans, retinal disease diagnosis from fundus images, real-time fetal monitoring using ultrasound and many more. In all these areas, deep learning enhances accuracy of diagnosis while decreasing time to interpretation, improve reproducibility, and contribute to early intervention strategies. To combat this issue, it has been shown that transfer learning, data augmentation and ensemble learning methods can significantly improve the performance of DL models, particularly when the size of the annotated data is limited which is often the case in most applications related to medical imaging. Despite some significant strides there are still major challenges to the translation of deep learning in the clinic. One of the principal issues is the interpretability, transparency of the DL models which can be characterized as the black box problem. © 2025 IEEE.
