AD  - The Ohio State University, Dept. of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Columbus, OH, United States
AB  - The assessment of wine quality is a complex task involving non-linear interactions between various physicochemical properties. While non-parametric methods like k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) offer intuitive classification logic, their performance significantly degrades in high-dimensional feature spaces due to the 'curse of dimensionality' and the uniform sensitivity of Euclidean distance to noisy features. Conversely, ensemble methods like Random Forest achieve high accuracy but often lack transparency, acting as 'black boxes' that obscure the decision-making process. To bridge the gap between performance and interpretability, we propose a hybrid Attention-KNN model. This architecture leverages a deep attention network not as a classifier, but as a supervised metric learning module that dynamically learns a weighted feature representation space. By assigning learnable importance weights to each physicochemical feature, our model effectively transforms the standard Euclidean distance into a semantic-aware metric. We validate our approach on the Wine Quality Dataset using a rigorous, reproducible experimental framework designed to prevent data leakage. Our results demonstrate that Attention-KNN achieves a test accuracy of 80.63 %, delivering performance comparable to a fully tuned Random Forest benchmark (82.19 %) while providing superior interpretability. Crucially, we validate the interpretability of our model through a rigorous bootstrap analysis, demonstrating that the learned attention weights are statistically stable and robust to data resampling. Through the visualization of learned attention weights, our method reveals key chemical determinants of wine quality, offering actionable insights for domain experts that traditional distance-based algorithms cannot provide.  © 2026 IEEE.
