AD  - School of Electrical Engineering and Telecommunications, University of New South Wales, Sydney, NSW, Australia
AD  - School of Robotics and Advanced Manufacturing, Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen, China
AD  - School of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, Nanyang Technological University, Singapore
AB  - Accurate short-term residential appliance load forecasting is critical for modern power system operation. Household electricity demand shows strong variability, nonstationarity and sudden fluctuations, which make accurate and interpretable forecasting challenging. This paper investigates interpretable deep learning approaches for short-term residential appliance load forecasting using a long short-term memory (LSTM) network explained by SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) and a Temporal Fusion Transformer (TFT) explained by built-in variable-selection and attention mechanisms. To ensure an objective-consistent point-forecasting comparison, an MSE-trained LSTM and an MSE-trained TFT are evaluated using the same data partition, feature-engineering procedure, feature-selection setting and evaluation protocol. Their point-forecasting performance is assessed using RMSE, MAPE and R2. In parallel, a quantile-trained TFT is evaluated separately for probabilistic forecasting using the mean pinball loss, approximate CRPS, prediction interval coverage probability and mean prediction interval width. Results show that the MSE-trained TFT achieves lower point-forecasting errors than the LSTM under the loss-aligned setting. The quantile-trained TFT further provides reasonably calibrated uncertainty estimates, with an 80% prediction interval coverage probability of 0.815 and an absolute coverage error of 0.015. In addition, the TFT provides feature-level and temporal explanations through its variable-selection and attention mechanisms, whereas SHAP offers complementary post-hoc explanations for the LSTM. The study provides a task-consistent comparison of recurrent and Transformer-based forecasting models and demonstrates how point accuracy, uncertainty quantification and interpretability can be jointly considered in residential load forecasting. © 2026 The Author(s). IET Smart Grid published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd on behalf of The Institution of Engineering and Technology.
