AB  - Optic disc edema (ODE) is an acute eye syndrome that needs to undergo proper grading in order to respond to the clinical intervention. The manual assessment system is time-consuming and has variability, and the current automated systems tend to be devoid of structural reasoning and their interpretability. The objective of the proposed study is to come up with a strong and justifiable hybrid model of accurate grading of ODE using fundus images. The suggested framework incorporates U-Net++ separate the optic discs, SCML to obtain morphological embeddings, R-GNN to learn relational structures, and a Vision Language Graph Transformer (VLGBT) to fusion and grading across modalities with the help of Normalized Attention Maps (NAM) to visualize the explanation. The structure was also evaluated using the Kaggle Identification of Pseudo papilledema dataset where the accuracy of 98.29 was obtained, as opposed to the traditional CNN, U-Net, and pure graph-based algorithms. According to qualitative evidence, the emphasis on crucial aspects that can be explained by clinicians is evident. The study concludes that such a hybrid approach not only ensures high accuracy in the grading process, but also reliability and utility in a real ophthalmic diagnostic process. © 2025 IEEE.
