TI  - SELS-Net: A Socio-Legal Deep Learning Framework for Evaluating the Status of Female Single Parents
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 2nd Asia Pacific Conference on Innovation in Technology, APCIT 2025
DO  - 10.1109/APCIT65661.2025.11411303
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035596685?origin=resultslist
AD  - Saveetha School of Law, Simats, Chennai, 77, India
AD  - Saveetha School of Law, Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences, Chennai, 77, India
AB  - This paper presents SELS-Net, a novel SocioLegal Deep Learning Framework designed to holistically evaluate the status of female single parents through a multimodal and multi-perspective approach. Recognizing the multifaceted vulnerabilities faced by this marginalized group - including legal neglect, economic instability, and social exclusion - SELS-Net integrates structured socioeconomic data, unstructured legal documents, and heterogeneous relational graphs into a unified analytical model. The architecture comprises three synergistic modules: a Bi-GRU-based Social Module to capture temporal patterns in welfare and demographic changes, a Transformer-based Legal Module to interpret legal narratives and judgments, and a Graph Neural Network (GNN) Module to analyze socio-legal relationships through a dynamic graph. These are fused through an attention-based mechanism that facilitates contextual decision-making and multi-label classification. SELS-Net was evaluated on a diverse dataset collected in collaboration with NGOs and legal aid organizations, annotated with risk categories such as "At Risk - No Legal Support", "Needs Immediate Legal Assistance", and "Legally Resolved - Vulnerable". The model achieved a superior accuracy of 93.87%, F1-score of 92.45%, and AUC of 96.12%, outperforming baselines like Bi-GRU, Legal-BERT, and traditional machine learning models. Additionally, SELS-Net demonstrated high interpretability through clause-level attention weights and maintained fairness across demographic subgroups such as SC/ST, religious minorities, and migrant populations. This framework provides actionable policy intelligence for social workers, legal practitioners, and welfare boards, enabling targeted intervention and rights-based governance. The SELS-Net model serves as a pioneering step in applying AI for equitable socio-legal decision-making. © 2025 IEEE.
