AB  - This study applied artificial intelligence algorithms to the time-series forecasting of meteorological variables associated with nocturnal drainage flows. Using observations from the Stable Atmospheric Variability And Transport (SAVANT) field campaign as the source data, a baseline dataset was constructed by extracting a core set of variables. These variables are output varibles. the XGBoost models were subsequently developed and integrated with SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) to quantify feature importance. Based on the resulting SHAP rankings, additional variables beyond the core were selected from the original dataset to construct additional data sets. Then six deep learning models were compared with the baseline and additional datasets. Prior to the experiments, the raw data were preprocessed and resampled to a uniform 5-minute temporal resolution. In this study, the multivariate time series forecasting task is defined as follows: a continuous 14-hour observation sequence (168 time steps at 5 -minute resolution) serves as input to predict the subsequent 6 time steps (30 minutes of future meteorological evolution). This forecast horizon closely matches operational requirements for short-term atmospheric prediction. Results indicate that the optimal forecasting performance was achieved using only the baseline dataset, whereas the inclusion of non-baseline variables reduced predictive accuracy. Among the six models, MTGNN achieved the lowest MSE, while Crossformer yielded the lowest MAE. The superior performance demonstrated by MTGNN and Crossformer suggests that future research can further investigate hybrid architectures integrating graph structure modeling and attention mechanisms, and provide novel insights and approaches for improving meteorological forecasting performance through additional fusion experiments. © 2026 IEEE.
