AB  - The agriculture domain contains various subcategories, and leaf diseases are among the most dominant and impactful challenges. The identification of leaf diseases faces challenges due to its complex disease characteristics, similar appearances among multiple classes, and the sensitivity of models relying on attention mechanisms. In this work, we introduce a Swin Transformer-based learning framework (ST-APCAR) that systematically enhances classification performance through four complementary mechanisms: Adaptive Learning Rate Scheduling, Pathway Regularization, Curriculum Learning, and Attention Norm Regularization. The proposed framework was developed as a coordinated training strategy in which each component addresses a specific weakness of transformer learning, including optimization instability, difficulty in progressive representation learning, unbalanced attention responses, and inefficient feature propagation for leaf disease classification. It investigates the effect of each proposed strategy independently to reveal its contribution to performance improvement and also validates the full ST-APCAR framework on two datasets using a common experimental setting. In the Agriculture Crops dataset, the proposed methods achieve 99.88% accuracy for Adaptive Learning Scheduling, 92.76% for Curriculum Learning, 91.92% for Pathway Regularization, and 92.76% for Attention Norm Regularization. The ST-APCAR architecture performed best with an accuracy of 99.92%. On the PlantDisease dataset, the models based on Adaptive Learning Scheduling, Curriculum Learning, Pathway Regularization, and Attention Norm Regularization achieve accuracy of 99.89%, 94.93%, 93.52%, and 96.92%, respectively. The ST-APCAR framework achieved 99.93% accuracy. Further, the superpixel-based score-drop analysis and Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) were used to analyze model predictions. These techniques highlight supporting and counter-evidence regions that drive classification decisions, and also provide both qualitative visualization and faithfulness assessment. The findings show that each proposed component improves the Swin Transformer, while their unified integration provides the most effective and consistent performance for leaf disease classification. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
