AB  - Arrhythmia refers to abnormal deviations in the normal heart rhythm, possibly signifying severe cardiovascular conditions. The manual identification of arrhythmias by ECG analysis is labor-intensive, requires specialized expertise, and is susceptible to inter-observer variability. Conventional signal-based classification techniques depend significantly on manually produced features, which may exhibit insufficient resilience and generalizability across varied patient demographics and noisy datasets. These constraints underscore the pressing need for automated, scalable, and interpretable diagnostic methodologies. To rectify these deficiencies, we offer an innovative approach that converts the ECG signal into 2D waveform images, facilitating the use of advanced image-based deep learning algorithms. ECG recordings from MIT-BIH were segmented around beats and converted to waveform graphs using wfdb, matplotlib, and PIL. Each image, reduced to 224 × 224 pixels, graphically encapsulates about 0.7 seconds of ECG data, enabling deep neural networks to learn both temporal and morphological features. The preprocessing pipeline comprises amplitude normalization, noise filtration and augmentation to improve signal quality and model robustness. We introduce a hybrid deep learning model (ECG-CNNTX -Electrocardiogram Classification using CNN and Transformer with Explainability) that integrates EfficientNetB0 for effective and resilient feature extraction with a Transformer-based attention module to capture long-range relationships in image representations. Our model achieved a classification accuracy of 99% in identifying Premature Ventricular Contractions (PVCs). Grad-CAM visualization elucidates essential ECG areas that impact predictions, hence augmenting clinical dependability for model interpretability. This study introduces a dependable, precise, and interpretable ECG classification framework that integrates traditional signal processing with sophisticated AI-driven image analysis, providing an innovative and scalable method for arrhythmia diagnosis. © 2013 IEEE.
