AD  - University of Petra, Software Engineering Department, Amman, Jordan
AD  - Al-Madinah College of Technology, Information Technology Department, Madinah, Saudi Arabia
AB  - The high velocity of memes related to hate speech spread on the Internet is a major challenge to automated content control systems. Their destructive significance is frequently based on the intricate combination of the image and text, so these memes cannot be classified using the models of classifications, which examine each modality separately. Recent multimodal models have been found to achieve better classification accuracy; but are commonly used as 'black boxes,' they are not transparent in their decision-making process. This paper presents a complete multimodal explainable AI (XAI) system that does not only classify memes as either hateful or non-hateful but also provides faithful, multimodal explanations of how the action came to this conclusion. The approach uses a Vision-and-Language Transformer (ViLT) model as well as novel explanatory mechanisms that take cross-modal attention patterns. An overall assessment of the Facebook Hateful Memes dataset indicates competitive predictive precision (accuracy = 0.742, AUC-ROC= 0.812) and quantitatively measures faithful explanations (Faithfulness = 0.234, AOPC = 0.156). The ablation test conducted with exhaustive analysis high maintaining the performance and explainability. The usefulness of the explanations is testified by human evaluation which involved 100 participants and showed high mean scores in plausibility (4.12/5.0) and helpfulness (4.28/5.0). This research, therefore, defines a confirmed route towards transparent, responsible and reliable AI systems in content moderation sphere. © 2026 IEEE.
