TI  - Ontology-Based Knowledge Graphs for Detecting Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs) With Neurosymbolic AI
PY  - 2025
T2  - IEEE International WIE Conference on Electrical and Computer Engineering/WIECON-ECE
IS  - 2025
SP  - 95
EP  - 100
DO  - 10.1109/WIECON-ECE69386.2025.11525900
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042669457?origin=resultslist
AD  - United International University, Department of Computer Science, Bangladesh
AB  - APTs are usually well-crafted multi-staged attacks that are low-signal and context dependant thereforethey can succeed in avoiding traditional intrusion detection system. In this paper, we propose a novel neurosymbolic AI framework that is composed of ontology-based KG and GNN toenhance the detection of APTs in enterprise networks. We create a cyber securityontology that represents APT tactics, techniques and procedures (TTPs) using notions from MITRE ATTCK and the Cyber Kill Chain, and we include in this representation explicit causal and temporal relationships between entities such as malicious infrastructure, hosts, processes, attack phases. Variety: Heterogeneous sources of data such as network logs and open-source threat intelligence (e.g., STIX/TAXII) are fed into a dynamic KG which facilitates context-awarereasoning. A heterogeneous GNN is further used to propagate node and edge level latent representations within this KG,but the symbolic rules learned from the ontology are leveraged to better refine and re-weight neural anomaly scores across plausible attack evolutions (e.g., 'reconnaissance → C2 communication'). This neurosymbolic fusion aims to solve the challenges over APT detection,i.e., scarcity of labeled data for zero-day attackers and no interpretability in black-box models. Our method obtains in both DARPA TRACE and MITRE ATTCK Evaluations the higher precision, recall, F1-score comparedto baselines (more than XGBoost and LSTM-based IDSs) generating interpretable alerts thanks to cause-and-effects chains. The framework is capable of near real-time operation and can be operationalised in security operations centres using widely-used large-scale graphanalytics libraries such as Neo4j and PyTorch Geometric, allowing users to scale out explainable APT detection as well as proactive threat hunting.  © 2025 IEEE.
