AB  - Uncovering causal relationships in electricity consumption time series is critical for optimizing energy management and informing industrial policy in the era of digital transformation and dual-carbon targets. Traditional causal discovery methods face significant limitations when applied to sectoral consumption data, as they typically rely on linearity and stationarity assumptions that fail to capture the complex, nonstationary dynamics inherent in modern electricity systems. To address these challenges, this paper develops a novel two-stage causal graph construction framework that progressively refines candidate relationships through correlation screening followed by frequency domain cosine similarity analysis, effectively filtering out spurious correlations while preserving genuine causal linkages. The method employs automatic binning for probability density estimation and enforces directed acyclic graph (DAG) structure through iterative cycle removal, effectively handling non-stationarity while quantifying directional influences with optimal time lags. We further employ SARIMAX prediction and PCMCI methods for comprehensive causal validation. Applied to actual data from ten consumption categories over 63 months in a certain province in southern China, our framework provides a causally interpretable representation of the complex temporal dependencies among electricity consumption sectors. This establishes the foundation for demand forecasting models and departmental coordination policies. © 2026 IEEE.
