AB  - The demand for the limited Radio Frequency (RF) spectrum has increased due to the exponential growth of wireless services for 5G, 6G, and the Internet of Things. Although Cognitive Radio (CR) provides a solution through Dynamic Spectrum Access (DSA), conventional sensing methods like Energy Detection and Cyclostationary Feature Detection are essentially constrained by the "SNR Wall,"which prevents them from reliably detecting signals in low Signal-to-Noise Ratio environments because of noise uncertainty. The transition from statistical techniques to sophisticated Deep Learning (DL) architectures for spectrum sensing is critically examined in this paper's systematic review of 35 research studies. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) for spatial feature extraction, recurrent neural networks (RNNs) for temporal modelling, and newly developed transformer-based hybrids are the three categories into which a thorough taxonomy is proposed. Additionally, by assessing new approaches in Explainable AI (XAI) for creating reliable, auditable sensing frameworks and Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) for augmenting synthetic data, the review tackles the crucial bottlenecks of data scarcity and model opacity. The synthesis shows that while XAI continues to be the crucial frontier for implementing black-box AI in safety-critical 6G networks, hybrid spatiotemporal architectures perform noticeably better than standalone models. © 2026 IEEE.
