AD  - State Key Laboratory of Synthetical Automation for Process Industries, Northeastern University, Shenyang, 110819, China
AD  - Departamento de Control Automatico, CINVESTAV-IPN (National Polytechnic Institute), Mexico, 07360, Mexico
AB  - This survey presents a neural-network-centric review of interactive explainability in data-driven modeling, covering mainstream architectures such as Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) and Transformers, with an emphasis on deployment requirements for safety, reliability, and compliance in high-risk settings. Black-box models trained solely to maximize predictive accuracy fall short of practical operational demands, and static explanations are insufficient to support online adjustment. To address this gap, we construct a unified four-level framework centered on Neural Networks (NNs), which categorizes interactive write-in points into data and knowledge, problem, model, and post-hoc levels. For each level, we summarize the interaction goals, write-in mechanisms, representative methods and explanation outputs, as well as evaluation and implementation considerations. In line with the theme of this special issue, we further review representative contributions of Professor Alexander Poznyak on Differential Neural Networks (DiNNs), Polynomial Neural Networks (PNNs) and Dynamic Neural Networks (DyNNs), as well as stability analysis. We reinterpret the feasible sets, error bounds, and stability certificates established in these works through the lens of interactive explainability, and use them to discuss the development potential of interactive explainability mechanisms on a theoretical basis. Finally, we summarize the key challenges and outline future directions for interactive explainability. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
