TI  - Explainable deep learning ensemble model for breast cancer classification in histopathology images
PY  - 2026
T2  - Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
VL  - 123
C7  - 110505
DO  - 10.1016/j.bspc.2026.110505
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037853249?origin=resultslist
AD  - Academy of Scientific and Innovative Research, Uttar Pradesh, Ghaziabad, India
AD  - Jyothy Institute of Technology, Thathaguni, Karnataka, Bangalore, 560082, India
AD  - CSIR Fourth Paradigm Institute, Karnataka, Bangalore, 560037, India
AB  - Breast cancer continues to be a major global issue, thus increasing the significance of early detection methods. While deep learning can automate image-based diagnosis by using convolutional neural networks (CNNs) in histopathology, their black-box nature and methodological issues including data leakage have limited their practical applications. In this study, we develop a new ensemble architecture that uses CNN (DenseNet121) and a vision transformer (ViT-L32) to capture local cellular and global tissue features respectively. Evaluations on the ICIAR 2018 BACH and BreakHis datasets were done. Importantly, in BreakHis dataset the images are at 200X magnification from 82 patients, stricter division of patient-level data was adopted to explicitly prevent data leakage. Our proposed accuracy-weighted ensemble performed better than either of its component models, yielding 91.00% accuracy on the BACH dataset. For the BreakHis dataset, considering the stringent patient-isolated testing framework, the ensemble achieved a clinical generalization accuracy of 81.48%, with an F1 score of 0.7885 and sensitivity of 97.25%, offering a very realistic evaluation compared to other reported studies. In addition, Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) and Integrated Gradient (IG) was used to shed light on the workings of the classifier. Faithfulness in the generated explanations was measured using a confidence drop metric based on the perturbation technique, and the results highlighted that the morphological regions have a causal relationship with the generated outcomes. Through a focus on strict segregation of data, the proposed CNN-Transformer ensemble can be regarded as a statistically validated and transparent AI tool for aiding breast cancer diagnosis. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
