TI  - Deep Learning Based Brain Tumor Size Estimation and Comparative Evaluation with Classical Algorithms
PY  - 2025
T2  - International Computer Conference on Wavelet Active Media Technology and Information Processing, ICCWAMTIP
DO  - 10.1109/ICCWAMTIP68645.2025.11352679
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033221008?origin=resultslist
AD  - School of Computer Science and Engineering, University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, Chengdu, 611731, China
AB  - This study explores the use of deep learning (DL) methods such as CNNs, U-Net variants, and transformer-based architectures for automated brain tumor segmentation and size quantification using multimodal MRI scans (Tl-weighted and FLAIR). Traditional approaches, which rely on handcrafted features and manual segmentation, suffer from observer variability and limited ability to capture tumor complexity. Our results show that DL models significantly outperform classical machine learning techniques (e.g., SVM, random forests) in accuracy, achieving Dice Similarity Coefficients (DSC) above 0.85, volumetric errors below 5%, and mean Hausdorff Distances near 4 mm. Ensemble DL models combining CNN, U-N et, and Transformer outputs further reduced volumetric errors to 3.8%, reflecting near-expert precision. Despite challenges like dataset limitations and interpretability, the study suggests future improvements using federated learning, multimodal data fusion, and explainable AI (XAI) to enhance clinical applicability. This research demonstrates the potential of DL frameworks to revolutionize brain tumor size estimation in neuro oncology, offering high reproducibility and volumetric precision for next-generation imaging systems.  © 2025 IEEE.
