AB  - Deep learning has significantly advanced robotic perception, enabling machines to recognize and interpret objects in ways that approximate human vision. However, these models remain limited by their “black-box” nature, making their decisions difficult to interpret and trust. This limitation is especially critical in robotics, where safety and reliability are essential. Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) seeks to address this challenge, yet evaluation remains constrained by the lack of ground-truth data. Collection and annotation of large-scale dataset, which also introduces explainability, is time-consuming and costly. To address these challenges, we present a diffusion-based framework for generating ground-truth visual explanation datasets. The resulting DXAI dataset contains 100,000 synthetic image–heatmap pairs across 100 household objects placed on flat surfaces, reflecting common robotic manipulation tasks such as grasping, sorting, and pick-and-place. We benchmark state-of-the-art attribution methods, both gradient and non-gradient-based, on Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Vision Transformers (ViTs). To assess explanation quality, we employ Intersection-Over-Union (IoU), Pointing Game (PG), and Insertion/Deletion AUC, measuring fidelity, localization accuracy, and causal impact. Our results highlight DXAI as a benchmark for advancing trustworthy robotic perception. The proposed dataset is publicly available at https://doi.org/10. 5281/zenodo.17197172 and the benchmarking suite is available at https://github.com/ ai-voyage/DXAI.git. © 2026, Australasian Robotics and Automation Association. All rights reserved.
