TI  - Explainable Deep Learning Framework for Drug-Target Interaction Prediction using Graph-Transformer Embeddings and Multi-Omics Integration
PY  - 2026
T2  - 6th International Conference on Computer Networks and Inventive Communication Technologies, ICCNCT 2026 - Proceedings
SP  - 1484
EP  - 1490
DO  - 10.1109/ICCNCT68477.2026.11590167
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045572199?origin=resultslist
AD  - St. Joseph's College Of Engineering, Information Technology, Chennai, India
AB  - Drug-target interaction (DTI) prediction is a vital aspect of computational drug discovery as it allows the identification of possible treatment candidates and the time and cost of experiment screens are minimized. Despite the recent success of deep learning techniques, most of the current models use only one modality of representation, or are black-box models with limited biological insight. To overcome these drawbacks, this paper suggests a hybrid deep learning model that includes the combination of graph neural networks to learn molecular structure and transformer-based encoders to learn protein sequences. A cross-interaction mechanism is employed to learn joint representations of drug-target pairs for binding affinity prediction. Besides this, explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) methods are also included to find influential substructures of molecules and protein residues, enhancing the transparency and reliability of models. The suggested framework is further tested on benchmark datasets, such as Davis and KIBA, to test its generalization ability. The outcomes of the experiments also show competitive predictive accuracy and significantly meaningful biological information, and suggest the future usefulness of the proposed method in making reliable computational drug discoveries. © 2026 IEEE.
