AD  - Institute of Smart City and Intelligent Transportation, Southwest Jiaotong University, Sichuan, Chengdu, China
AD  - Cho Chun Shik Graduate School of Mobility, Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology, South Korea
AD  - School of Traffic and Transportation, Lanzhou Jiaotong University, Gansu, Lanzhou, China
AB  - Driver fatigue remains a pressing global road safety concern, particularly among young drivers. It contributes to an estimated 14%-20% of all traffic accidents worldwide, yet real-time and policy-relevant detection systems remain underdeveloped. To address this challenge, this study proposes SET-DGCN-an end-to-end EEG-based fatigue detection model that combines a convolution-enhanced Transformer with a dynamic graph convolutional network (DGCN). The model captures multi-scale temporal dependencies and spatial brain-region interactions by integrating convolutional embeddings, hybrid attention mechanisms, and learnable graph structures. To enhance model transparency and real-world applicability, this study adopts a dual-explanation strategy: COAR is employed to quantify the contribution of individual model components, while SHAP analysis reveals brain region-specific patterns across fatigue stages. The model is evaluated on both a self-collected driving simulation dataset and the public SEED-VIG dataset and consistently outperforms leading CNN-, GCN-, and Transformer-based baselines in terms of classification accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score, while demonstrating strong cross-subject generalization. These insights are translated into a multi-level policy framework, encompassing infrastructure enhancements, adaptive in-vehicle interventions, and personalized fatigue mitigation strategies. By bridging neuroscience, deep learning, and transportation safety policy, this study supports scalable, interpretable, and policy-aware solutions for enhancing road safety in both conventional and emerging intelligent transportation systems. © HKSTS 2025: Smart Mobility.All rights reserved.
