AD  - The University of Tennessee At Chattanooga, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Chattanooga, TN, United States
AB  - Machine learning based vulnerability detection has emerged as a promising approach for improving software security at scale, particularly in domains where manual analysis is costly or impractical. Recent advances in transformer-based models, such as CodeBERT, have demonstrated strong performance on vulnerability detection benchmarks by leveraging contextual representations learned from large corpora of source code. However, the opacity of these models raises concerns about their suitability for security-critical applications, where practitioners must understand and trust model decisions. in contrast, simpler linear models using interpretable features provide transparent explanations but are often assumed to offer inferior detection performance. This paper presents a systematic comparison between an explainable baseline model based on TF-IDF character ngrams with Logistic Regression and a transformer-based model, CodeBERT, for vulnerability detection in IoT-oriented C/C++ code using the IoTvulCode dataset. in addition to standard detection metrics, we evaluate explanation faithfulness and robustness using insertion and deletion tests. Our experimental results show that although CodeBERT significantly outperforms the explainable baseline in detection accuracy, its explanations exhibit weak faithfulness under perturbation-based evaluation. Conversely, the explainable baseline produces substantially more faithful and stable explanations despite lower overall detection performance. These findings highlight a fundamental trade-off between accuracy and explanation reliability and underscore the importance of incorporating faithfulness evaluation when deploying machine learning models for vulnerability detection in security-critical environments. © 2026 IEEE.
